# UST.G3FV Selected Cox Hilbert--Burch Source Theorem

## Scope

UST.G3FK writes the selected K3, elliptic and eta9 equations as contractions
of one four-normal row. G3FV asks the immediate upstream question left open by
the finite G3FN theorem: does that selected source itself carry a
Hilbert--Burch object, before any finite interpolation or RREF normalization?

The answer is yes at the Cox-homogeneous level. Equality with the finite
component-20 apolar complex remains a separate comparison theorem.

## The source matrix

Transpose the exact G3FK coefficient matrix and write

```text
      K3       E          F
q   [ H4       0        t0 D7 ]
r   [ rbar     0        t0 B6 ]
ell [ 0        qe       A1    ]
t2  [ 0        t2^2     A2    ].
```

This is a `4 x 3` Cox-homogeneous matrix `S` over `Q(gamma)`. Its rows are the
four selected normals and its columns are the three selected equations.

Set

```text
C_E = qe A2 - t2^2 A1,
C_K = t0 (H4 B6 - rbar D7).
```

The signed maximal minors are exactly

```text
Delta = (rbar C_E, -H4 C_E, -t2^2 C_K, qe C_K),
```

and direct expansion gives `S^T Delta = 0`.

## Height two

The pairs `(H4,rbar)` and `(qe,t2^2)` are coprime. A common divisor of `C_E`
and `C_K` would therefore have to be either `t0`, which does not divide
`C_E`, or a base-variable divisor common to `A1` and `A2`.

The latter possibility is excluded by a good-reduction certificate. At
`p=21817`, `gamma=5`, restrict weighted-homogeneously by

```text
x=s, y=t, z=s+2t, w=s^3+2st^2+t^3.
```

Both selected forms remain degree nine after setting `s=1`, and their gcd in
`F_21817[t]` is one. A nonconstant weighted-homogeneous common factor over
`Q(gamma)` would have a nonzero positive-degree reduction on this graded test
curve, contradicting that gcd.

Consequently the four maximal minors have gcd one. Their proper determinantal
ideal has height at most two by the determinantal height bound and cannot lie
in a height-one prime. Its height is therefore exactly two.

## Theorem

Hilbert--Burch now gives the exact complex

```text
0 -> R^3 --S--> R^4 --Delta^T--> I_3(S) -> 0.
```

Thus the four-generator/three-syzygy architecture is derived directly from
the one selected G3FK source. It is no longer merely a numerical shape seen
in G3FN.

## What remains open

G3FV does **not** identify the entries of `S` with the normalized finite G3FN
matrix. The finite matrix lives after Cox/H02 residue projection and
divided-power apolar duality; its column degrees are `1,2,8`. The next theorem
must construct that functor and prove that the image of this exact Cox complex
is the G3FN complex, equivalently the G3FR mixed-degree inverse source
`18@15 + 1@16 + 1@19`.

No component-20 rank-36 promotion, global H02 equality or physical endpoint
is claimed here.

